You ignore one situation under which things like dwarvish helms, iron
shoes &c aren't that plentiful--if you're a lawful of a race to which
dwarves are (or tend to be) peaceful. If you're a dwarf yourself,
getting dwarvish armor is (ironically) rather difficult, unless you
like annoying your god.

Of course, in this discussion we're assuming you're using a ?oEA to
fooproof (NB: that method also fixes damage!). I doubt it'd ever be
wasteful to fooproof a good bit of armor if you're using cursed scrolls
of destroy armor (as long as it's not damaged first!).

I tend to assume that anything that can be rusted will become
rusted--thus, if I rustproof a dwarvish iron helm, in the long run I've
effectively enchanted it twice, once I run into a rust monster. And
climbing all the way up from Gehennom to replace a &@*#$%\ helm that
rusted is so annoying.

Also, keep in mind that fooproofing can only be removed by some dumb
action on the player's part--a bad run-in with a disenchanter can
remove any evidence the thing was ever +5.

I'd say, in the long run, fooproofing is a safer investment, *if the
armor is something you'll keep*. For instance, fireproofing speed boots
is probably a good investment.

In the short run, especially in the early game, enchanting is probably
more effective, since, in the short run, you're probably not going to
enchant it to +5 anyhow (which would be the only reason to then worry
about the base AC you're losing).



